artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{SM}}$ denote the Lagrangian of the Standard Model of particle physics, formulated as a renormalizable gauge theory with gauge group $SU(3)_C 	imes SU(2)_L 	imes U(1)_Y$, three generations of quarks and leptons, and a single Higgs doublet. In $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{SM}}$ the neutrinos are exactly massless. Experiments observing neutrino flavor oscillations show that at least two of the three light neutrinos have nonzero masses and that the leptonic charged-current mixing matrix (the PMNS matrix) is nontrivial.

Consider extensions $\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{SM}} + \Delta\mathcal{L}$ that produce three light neutrino mass eigenstates with masses $m_1, m_2, m_3$ and a unitary (or, more generally, possibly non-unitary) leptonic mixing matrix. Standard candidate forms of $\Delta\mathcal{L}$ include, among others:
(i) the Weinberg dimension-five operator $ rac{c_{\alpha eta}}{\Lambda}(\overline{L_\alpha^c}	ilde H^*)(	ilde H^\dagger L_ eta)$ giving Majorana masses;
(ii) type-I seesaw with right-handed singlet neutrinos $N_R$ and Yukawa/Majorana terms $-y_{\alpha i}\overline{L_\alpha}	ilde H N_{Ri} - 	frac12 M_{ij}\overline{N_{Ri}^c}N_{Rj} + \mathrm{h.c.}$;
(iii) type-II seesaw with a scalar $SU(2)_L$ triplet of hypercharge $1$;
(iv) type-III seesaw with fermion $SU(2)_L$ triplets;
(v) inverse and linear seesaw variants with additional singlets;
(vi) radiative (loop-induced) neutrino mass models;
(vii) Dirac-mass scenarios with new chiral singlets and an exact lepton-number symmetry;
(viii) models invoking $R$-parity violation, extra dimensions, or other beyond-Standard-Model structures.

Problem. Determine which choice of $\Delta\mathcal{L}$ is realized in Nature. Specifically:
(a) Is the light-neutrino mass term Majorana or Dirac?
(b) What is the absolute mass scale and ordering (normal vs. inverted) of $m_1, m_2, m_3$?
(c) What are the values of all CP-violating phases in the leptonic mixing matrix?
(d) Identify the underlying high-scale (or low-scale) mechanism of mass generation, and specify a complete set of experimental observables (e.g., neutrinoless double beta decay rates, kinematic endpoint measurements, cosmological mass bounds, searches for heavy neutral leptons, charged lepton flavor violation, non-unitarity tests, oscillation precision data) sufficient to discriminate the realized $\Delta\mathcal{L}$ from all competing renormalizable or effective-theory alternatives.
